    "last_text": "\u0001\n850 \nFXUS64 KHGX 031007\nAFDHGX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Houston/Galveston TX\n507 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\n...New AVIATION...\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Couldn't rule out patchy dense fog this morning. \n\n- Hot summer weather through the forecast period. \n\n- Isolated afternoon shower/storm possible first half of week.\n  Somewhat better chance of an afternoon shower/storm later in the\n  week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nIssued at 1116 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nA mid/upper ridge over SW CONUS is amplifying northeastward, with\nits axis extending into the northern U.S. plains and central \nCanada. A deep-layer trough remains in place over E CONUS, with \nvery dry mid/upper air pushing southward between the trough and \nthe ridge. Even at the surface, very low dew points have pushed \ninto our region in the wake of relatively weak sfc cold front. The\nfront has stalled near the coast, becoming increasingly diffused.\nWe'll likely continue the dry LL regime on Monday, featuring \nlow/mid 60s dew points north of I-10 and 70s dew points near the \ncoast. However,the sea breeze boundary is expected to push further\ninland Monday afternoon, bringing higher dew points farther \ninland later in the day. Couldn't rule out an isolated afternoon \nshower or storm along the sea breeze boundary. We've actually \ndialed back on the hot temperatures a tad, making me glad we \ndidn't totally buy into those 102-104 degree temperatures the NBM \nwas suggesting a few days ago. But we are still calling for \ntemperatures near 100 degrees inland, and low 90s at the coast. If\nthe sea breeze is delayed, then we can get a little hotter. \n\nThe mid/upper flow pattern is expected to become less amplified as \nthe week progresses. The E CONUS trough should erode north, while\nthe ridge to our west expands, increasing mid/upper pressure \nheights over Texas. Global ensemble mean 500 MB heights shows \nvalues increasing from roughly ~588dam to ~594dam between now and \nweek's end. One's initial reaction to this trend would be the \nexpectation of building heat. But as the E CONUS troughing fades \naway, our LL pattern will become more influenced by high pressure \nto the east, establishing deep onshore flow. This will have a \ntendency to increase the chance of late morning and afternoon \ncumulus, and could potentially increase the risk of a few \nisolated afternoon showers/storms. Therefore, afternoon high \ntemperatures are actually forecast to decrease somewhat later this\nweek (still quite hot in the mid 90s). However, persistent \nonshore flow will likely increase dew points as well. So expect an\nincreasingly humid environment as the week progresses. Global \nensembles keep PWAT anomalies below average, thus the PoPs \nremaining low all week. \n\nSelf\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...\n(12Z TAF Issuance)\nIssued at 507 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nLight and variable winds will persist through most of the morning\nbefore becoming southerly to southeasterly in the afternoon hours.\nTerminals closer to the coast will start out with light south-\nsouthwesterly winds before transitioning to southeasterly winds\naround 10 kt this afternoon. Cannot rule out intermittent periods\nof patchy fog over the next few hours this morning in LBX/SGR. \nIsolated SH/TS possible this afternoon around the Houston metro \nterminals between 20Z-01Z, but the potential is too low to include\nit in any of the TAFs. Brief periods of patchy fog will be \npossible again early Tuesday morning. \n\nBatiste\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nIssued at 1116 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nLight to moderate winds and low seas are expected through the\nforecast period. Winds on Monday are expected to be west to\nsouthwest, before becoming more southerly as we approach mid week.\nBy the end of the week, winds are expected to become more\nsoutheasterly. Isolated shower or storm possible along the sea\nbreeze boundary during the first half of the week. Somewhat better\nchance of a shower/storm in the afternoon for the bays, and in the\nmorning hours in the Gulf later in the week.\n\n&&\n\n.PRELIMINARY POINT TEMPS/POPS...\nCollege Station (CLL)\t 100  75  98  76 /  10   0   0   0 \nHouston (IAH)\t\t\t 100  79  99  79 /  20   0  10  10 \nGalveston (GLS)\t\t\t  91  83  91  83 /  10   0   0   0 \n\n&&\n\n.HGX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nTX...None.\nGM...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Self\nAVIATION...Batiste\nMARINE...Self\n\n\u0003\n",
    "send_day": "2026-08-03",
